# Renaming Styles Across Multiple Documents

Updating style properties is not the only situation where a large document collection can create repetitive work.

Sometimes the style definitions are acceptable, but the naming convention needs to change.

For example:

```text
Old naming

Title
Subtitle
Body
Caption
```

might need to become:

```text
New naming

Heading 1
Heading 2
Body Text
Image Caption
```

Changing these names across a large document collection manually can become a substantial production task.

The same applies to:

* Paragraph Styles;
* Character Styles;
* Object Styles.

When the same renaming operation needs to be applied repeatedly, batch automation can reduce the amount of manual work.

---

# What If Two Documents Have the Same Style Name?

Another common production situation occurs when two documents contain a style with the same name but different properties.

For example:

```text
Document A

Body Text
12 pt
15 pt leading
```

while another document contains:

```text
Document B

Body Text
11 pt
14 pt leading
```

The style name is identical.

The definition is not.

This can create inconsistencies that are difficult to identify when working across a large document collection.

A reference document can therefore be useful when the objective is to establish a consistent style definition.

---

# Synchronizing Styles From a Reference Document

A common production model is to designate one document as the reference.

For example:

```text
Reference.indd

        ↓

┌───────────────────────┐
│ Chapter-01.indd       │
│ Chapter-02.indd       │
│ Chapter-03.indd       │
│ Chapter-04.indd       │
│ Chapter-05.indd       │
└───────────────────────┘
```

The reference document contains the approved style definitions.

The objective is then to bring the other documents into alignment with that reference.

For smaller projects, this can be handled using InDesign's native tools.

For larger collections, the repetitive nature of the operation is where batch automation can become useful.

---

# When Does Batch Automation Make Sense?

There is no reason to automate every style change.

If you need to update one document, doing it manually may be faster.

If you need to update a handful of documents, InDesign's native tools may be sufficient.

The situation changes when the same operation needs to be repeated across a large document collection.

For example:

| Situation                      | Recommended approach      |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| One document                   | Manual editing            |
| A few documents                | Native InDesign tools     |
| Documents organized as a Book  | Book synchronization      |
| Many independent documents     | Consider batch processing |
| Repeated style renaming        | Batch workflow            |
| Repeated style synchronization | Batch workflow            |
| Large document collections     | Automated processing      |

The appropriate method depends on the size and structure of the project.

